<ContentPage
  title="Project Origin — CodeXX DTDK"
  description="How CodeXX DTDK began: a documentation problem on a hobby game engine that led, through Clang and regex dead-ends, to a tree-sitter–based engine for understanding C++."
  eyebrow="Project Origin"
  heading="It started with documentation."
  lead="CodeXX DTDK didn't begin as a platform. It began as one stubborn problem on a side project — and the long road to solving it properly."
  active="origin"
>
  <p>
    In my free time I was building a game engine. Like every engine, it grew a
    surface area faster than I could document it — and I wanted documentation
    that actually looked modern: something that would drop cleanly into an
    <strong>Astro + Starlight</strong> site, with real cross-references and code
    navigation rather than a wall of extracted comments.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nothing off the shelf did what I wanted, so I set out to generate it from
    source. That turned out to be the hard part.
  </p>

  <h2>The two dead-ends</h2>

  <p>
    My first instinct was to reach for <strong>Clang</strong>. If anything
    understands C++, it's the compiler front-end. But building tooling on top of
    a compiler is heavy: slow to set up, awkward to embed, and shaped around
    compilation rather than around <em>understanding</em> a codebase the way a
    human reader does.
  </p>

  <p>
    So I swung the other way — <strong>heuristics and regular expressions</strong>.
    Fast to start, and just as fast to fall apart. C++ is far too context-sensitive
    to pattern-match your way through; every rule I added spawned three exceptions.
  </p>

  <p class="pull">
    The answer was in the middle ground.</p>

  <h2>The middle ground</h2>

  <p>
    Between the compiler's weight and the regex's fragility sat
    <strong>tree-sitter</strong> — fast, incremental, embeddable, and forgiving of
    incomplete code. On its own it produces a concrete syntax tree, not the
    semantic picture I needed. So I forked it, updated it for the C++ I cared
    about, and started building my own <strong>AST and analysis layer on top of
    its CST</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>
    That layer is what became the CodeXX engine. Once you own the whole path from
    source to a real understanding of the code, documentation stops being the
    goal and becomes just the first thing you can build. Code generation,
    memory-safety analysis, IDE intelligence — they all fall out of the same
    foundation.
  </p>

  <p>
    The documentation generator that started it all still ships today. It just
    turned out to be the smallest piece of a much larger idea.
  </p>
</ContentPage>
